No. This does not establish that App Z caused people to feel better.

What the evidence supports:

Among the 80 surveyed users, 43 people (53.75%) reported feeling better after using App Z.
It describes users’ reported experiences at the time of the survey.
What it does not establish:

Whether those people were already feeling better before using the app, because no pre-use measurements were taken.
Whether their improvement was caused by App Z rather than by placebo effects, outside events, natural recovery, or other factors.
Whether users who did not feel better were underrepresented or less likely to respond.
Whether App Z performs better than doing nothing or using another app, because there was no control group.
The result is descriptive evidence of a reported association, not evidence of a causal effect.


